=95=90 -->
  <div class=3D"section highlight">
    <h2><span class=3D"icon">=F0=9F=93=8C</span> What is the Context Window=
?</h2>
    <p>
      Every time an LLM processes a request, it reads a finite block of tex=
t - the context window.
      Everything the model "knows" during that call comes from exactly two =
sources:
      its <strong>pre-training weights</strong> (frozen, general knowledge)=
 and the
      <strong>context window</strong> (dynamic, call-specific information y=
ou supply).
    </p>
    <p>
      As an AI engineer, you have no control over the weights. The context =
window is your <em>entire design surface</em>.
    </p>
    <p>  =20
      The context window is the maximum number of tokens an LLM can read in=
 a single forward pass.
      Tokens include <em>everything</em>: system prompt, conversation histo=
ry, retrieved documents,
      tool call results, and the model's own generated response.
    </p>

    <p style=3D"margin-top:0.5rem">
      =E2=9A=A0 <strong>Tokens in the output count too</strong> - every tok=
en the model generates reduces
      the space available for input on the next turn. A long response shrin=
ks your remaining context budget.
    </p>
  </div>

