  <div class=3D"section">
    <h2><span class=3D"icon">=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F</span> Lost in the Middle P=
roblem</h2>
    <p>
      LLMs exhibit a <strong>"lost in the middle"</strong> behavior - infor=
mation at the beginning and end of a long
      context is utilized more effectively than information buried in the m=
iddle. Retrieval accuracy follows a
      <strong>U-shaped curve</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class=3D"formula">Accuracy

High  =E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=95=AE      =E2=95=AD=E2=94=80=E2=94=80
         =E2=95=B2    =E2=95=B1
          =E2=95=B2  =E2=95=B1
Low        =E2=95=B0=E2=95=AF
      Start   Middle   End</div>

    <p style=3D"margin-top:0.75rem">
      Reference: <em>"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Cont=
exts"</em> (Liu et al.) - models performed
      best when relevant information appeared near the start or end, and wo=
rst when it appeared in the middle.
    </p>

    <hr class=3D"divider">

    <h3>1. Recency Effect (End of Context)</h3>
    <p>
      The end of the context is given higher importance because of how tran=
sformer-based generation works.
      When generating the next token, the model attends to all previous tok=
ens, but the most recent tokens
      are closer to the current generation step and easier to utilize. This=
 effect is driven by
      <strong>transformer architecture and generation dynamics</strong>.
    </p>
    <div class=3D"formula">[ Long Context ]
[ Long Context ]
[ Long Context ]

User Question    =E2=86=90 appears at the end =E2=86=92 highly influential<=
/div>

    <hr class=3D"divider">

    <h3>2. Primacy Effect (Beginning of Context)</h3>
    <p>
      The beginning-of-context advantage is <strong>not</strong> built into=
 the transformer architecture - it
      emerges from training. During pretraining, documents frequently start=
 with titles, headings, introductions,
      and summaries. During instruction tuning, prompts often begin with:
    </p>
    <div class=3D"formula">You are a helpful assistant.
Follow these rules...</div>
    <p>
      As a result, the model learns that early tokens often contain critica=
l information.
      This effect is primarily a <strong>learned behavior</strong> rather t=
han an architectural property.
    </p>

    <hr class=3D"divider">

    <h3>3. Why is the Middle Weaker?</h3>
    <p>As context length increases:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Attention must be distributed across more tokens</li>
      <li>Relevant information competes with irrelevant information</li>
      <li>Retrieval of information from the middle becomes less reliable</l=
i>
    </ul>
    <p style=3D"margin-top:0.5rem">
      The information is not forgotten, but the probability of successfully=
 utilizing it decreases
      compared to information near the beginning or end.
    </p>
